<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southampton</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Southampton is a port city on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, at the head of Southampton Water. In the eighth century the trading settlement of Hamwic on its site was one of the main emporia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> England, dealing with the Frankish world, and the later town gave its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>. A leading medieval and modern port, it was the departure point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower">Mayflower</a> in 1620 and the Titanic in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southampton.jpg" alt="Southampton" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Martin · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_across_Ocean_Village_Marina_(geograph_5432669).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological finds suggest the area has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>. After the Roman invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 43 and the conquest of the local Britons in 70, a fortress settlement named Clausentum was established at the site of modern Bitterne Manor. It was an important trading port and defensive outpost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, defended by a wall and two ditches, and is thought to have had a bath house. Clausentum was not abandoned until around 410.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066 Southampton became the major port of transit between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, then the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and Normandy. Southampton Castle was built in the 12th century, and the surviving remains of merchants' houses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a>'s House and Canute's Palace show the wealth of the town at that time. By the 13th century it was a leading port, importing French wine in exchange for English cloth and wool. French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> raided it, which led to the building of the fortified town walls, many of which still stand.</p><p>In 1415, before Henry V left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Battle of Agincourt</a>, the ringleaders of the Southampton Plot, Richard, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, Henry Scrope, 3rd Baron Scrope of Masham, and Sir Thomas Grey of Heton, were accused of high treason, tried at what is now the Red Lion public house in the High Street and executed outside the Bargate. The friary was dissolved in 1538. The Pilgrim Fathers set out from the port aboard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayflower">Mayflower</a> in 1620, though the ship was forced to return to Plymouth. In 1642, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, a Parliamentary garrison moved in, and the Royalists, who advanced as far as Redbridge in March 1644, were prevented from taking the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town expanded greatly in the Victorian era. The Southampton Docks company was formed in 1835, the foundation stone of the docks was laid in October 1838, the first dock opened in 1842, and the railway link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was fully opened in May 1840. It became known as The Gateway to the Empire. In 1912 the RMS Titanic sailed from Southampton; 497 men, four in five of the crew on board, were Sotonians. The city was heavily bombed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and was a major embarkation point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">D-Day</a>. Southampton acquired city status in 1964, had a population of 248,922 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and has the longest surviving stretch of medieval walls in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Southampton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade flowed through the emporium of Hamwic, on the site of <b>Southampton</b>, which flourished in the eighth century and dealt with the Frankish world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They were sent to <b>Southampton</b> in 1861 to 1863 for photozincographic reproduction, and evacuated to Bodmin Prison during the First World War and to Shepton Mallet Prison during the Second World War.</blockquote><a href="/work/domesday-book">Domesday Book · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>RMS Titanic, the largest ship afloat, sailed from <b>Southampton</b> for New York via Cherbourg and Queenstown.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-10">10 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
